  "response": "The Federalist, the series of eighty-five essays published in New York newspapers in 1787 and 1788 under the shared pseudonym Publius, became one of the most closely studied problems of attribution in American letters. The authorship of twelve Federalist essays was disputed between Hamilton and Madison, and neither man left the question settled. Although Hamilton left a list before his 1804 duel that attributed the disputed essays to himself, Madison later claimed authorship of them. Each statesman had a strong claim to credibility, each had written prolifically in the same genre, and the two had deliberately adopted a common voice, so internal evidence alone could not readily separate them. For more than a century, partisans of each attribution read the same essays and reached opposite conclusions.\n\nHistorical scholarship moved the debate before quantitative methods entered it. Historian Douglass Adair argued for Madison's authorship of the disputed essays before Mosteller and Wallace published their findings in the book \"Inference and Disputed Authorship: The Federalist\" in 1964. Adair drew on political argument, correspondence, and the circumstances of composition, but his case remained interpretive and open to rejoinder. Mosteller and Wallace published their authorship study in 1975, framing it as a statistical demonstration rather than a historical judgment. Their central methodological choice was decisive: Mosteller and Wallace focused their analysis on function words rather than content words. Content words track subject matter, and since Hamilton and Madison addressed the same constitutional questions, vocabulary of that kind offered little discrimination. Words such as articles, prepositions, and conjunctions, however, are used largely without conscious control and vary only slightly with topic.\n\nSeveral such words proved to be markers of individual style. At least one of two habits distinguishes the authors, perhaps both: Hamilton habitually used the word \"upon\" in his prose, or Madison habitually used the word \"whilst\" in his prose. The word \"upon\" appeared far more frequently in Madison's writings than in Hamilton's. Markers of this sort, together with dozens of less conspicuous words such as \"by,\" \"to,\" and \"also,\" supplied the raw counts from which the investigators built their models. Because any single word could appear or vanish in a given essay by chance, the counts had to be combined across many words and many texts of known authorship before they could support a firm verdict on any contested paper.\n\nBecause of that focus on function words, Mosteller and Wallace's analysis relied on Bayes' theorem; in other words, they used Bayesian inference to weigh the authorship evidence. The procedure began with prior odds between the two candidates and revised those odds according to how probable each essay's observed word frequencies would be under Hamilton's habits and under Madison's. The investigators modeled word rates with negative binomial and Poisson distributions estimated from essays whose authorship was certain, and they tested how sensitive their results were to different choices of prior. The resulting odds were so lopsided that even sharply unfavorable starting assumptions could not reverse them.\n\nThe outcome admitted only two possibilities: either Mosteller and Wallace attributed all twelve disputed essays to Madison, or they attributed at least one disputed essay to someone other than Madison; the two cannot both be true, and one of them must hold. Reviewers who later re-examined the study's published tables determined that Mosteller and Wallace had in fact assigned every disputed paper to Madison, settling which of the two possibilities held. Their conclusion that Madison wrote Federalist Nos. 49 through 58, 62, and 63 confirms the first of these, since those numbers comprise the entire disputed set. Madison was credited with those essays because the statistical analysis ultimately determined that Hamilton's list was wrong to credit him with the disputed essays. That determination resolved the tension between Hamilton's pre-duel list and Madison's later claim, settling in Madison's favor a dispute that documentary evidence had left open.",
